10 Best Node.js Security Practices for Production
A practical production security guide for Node.js and Express applications
Production security should be implemented as a defense-in-depth strategy across application code, APIs, authentication, dependencies, infrastructure, and monitoring.
1. Keep Node.js and Dependencies Updated
Use a supported Node.js LTS release and regularly scan and update dependencies.
Example commands:
node -v
npm outdated
npm audit
npm audit fix
For Yarn:
yarn outdated
yarn audit
Best practices:
• Use an actively supported Node.js version.
• Remove unused dependencies.
• Review security advisories before upgrades.
• Commit lock files such as package-lock.json or yarn.lock.
• Automate dependency scanning in CI/CD.
Why it matters: Vulnerable third-party packages can become a major attack surface.


2. Never Hard-Code Secrets
Never place passwords, API keys, JWT secrets, or database credentials directly in source code.
Avoid:
const jwtSecret = "my-super-secret-key";
Use environment variables:
const jwtSecret = process.env.JWT_SECRET;
const databaseUrl = process.env.DATABASE_URL;
Example .env:
JWT_SECRET=very-long-random-secret
DATABASE_URL=mongodb://...
Add environment files to .gitignore:
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
For production, prefer a dedicated secrets manager such as AWS Secrets Manager or Parameter Store.


3. Implement Strong Authentication and Authorization
Authentication establishes who the user is; authorization determines what the user is allowed to do.
JWT example:
const token = jwt.sign(
  { userId: user.id, role: user.role },
  process.env.JWT_SECRET!,
  { expiresIn: "15m" }
);
Production recommendations:
• Use short-lived access tokens.
• Implement a secure refresh-token strategy.
• Apply role-based access control (RBAC).
• Hash passwords with Argon2id or bcrypt.
• Use MFA for sensitive operations.
• Never trust authorization data supplied directly by the client.


4. Validate and Sanitize User Input
Never assume incoming request data is safe. Validate body, query, path parameters, headers, and uploaded data.
Joi example:
const schema = Joi.object({
  firstName: Joi.string().trim().min(2).max(50).required(),
  email: Joi.string().email().required(),
  age: Joi.number().integer().min(18)
});
Then validate before processing:
const { error, value } = schema.validate(req.body);
Input validation helps protect against malformed requests, injection attacks, unexpected data types, and excessively large inputs.


5. Protect Against SQL and NoSQL Injection
Avoid constructing database queries from untrusted input.
For MongoDB, validate types and prevent clients from supplying unexpected operators such as $ne, $gt, or $where.
For SQL, use parameterized queries:
await db.query(
  "SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = $1",
  [email]
);
Never build SQL using string concatenation:
`SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '${email}'`
Use an ORM/query builder carefully and validate the application's business inputs.


6. Secure HTTP Headers
For Express applications, use Helmet to configure security-related HTTP headers.
Install:
npm install helmet
Usage:
import helmet from "helmet";
app.use(helmet());
Also disable Express fingerprinting:
app.disable("x-powered-by");
Security headers can help with protections involving Content Security Policy, content sniffing, HSTS, referrer policy, and other browser security controls.


7. Configure CORS Properly
Avoid unrestricted CORS in production.
Avoid:
app.use(cors({ origin: "*" }));
Prefer an explicit allow-list:
const allowedOrigins = [
  "https://app.example.com",
  "https://staging.example.com"
];
app.use(cors({
  origin(origin, callback) {
    if (!origin || allowedOrigins.includes(origin)) {
      callback(null, true);
    } else {
      callback(new Error("Not allowed by CORS"));
    }
  }
}));
Important: CORS is not an authentication mechanism. It mainly controls browser cross-origin access.


8. Add Rate Limiting
Protect login, OTP, password-reset, payment, and other abuse-prone endpoints.
Install:
npm install express-rate-limit
Example:
const loginLimiter = rateLimit({
  windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000,
  limit: 5,
  standardHeaders: "draft-8",
  legacyHeaders: false,
  message: { message: "Too many login attempts" }
});
app.use("/api/auth/login", loginLimiter);
For multiple Node.js instances, use a shared store such as Redis so rate limits are consistent across instances.


9. Use HTTPS and Secure Cookies
Production APIs should use HTTPS, typically terminated at an Nginx reverse proxy, load balancer, or API gateway.
For authentication cookies:
res.cookie("refreshToken", token, {
  httpOnly: true,
  secure: true,
  sameSite: "strict",
  maxAge: 7 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000
});
Important attributes:
• HttpOnly — reduces JavaScript access to the cookie.
• Secure — sends the cookie only over HTTPS.
• SameSite — helps mitigate CSRF in appropriate architectures.
• Appropriate expiration — limits exposure if a token is compromised.


10. Logging, Monitoring, and Error Handling
Security requires visibility into failures and suspicious behavior.
Avoid logging sensitive values such as passwords, JWTs, API keys, database credentials, payment information, and session cookies.
Prefer structured logs:
logger.error({
  requestId,
  route: req.originalUrl,
  method: req.method,
  statusCode: 500,
  errorCode: "DB_CONNECTION_ERROR"
});
A typical production monitoring flow is:
Node.js → Winston/Pino → CloudWatch Logs → Metric Filters → Metrics → CloudWatch Alarms → SNS/Email
Useful alert categories include repeated authentication failures, database errors, payment failures, rate-limit events, and excessive 5xx responses.
